body:
Let's think this through...

https://github.com/rclone/rclone/blob/cce399515fbe58d91584b932218e5aaf899eb661/cmd/bisync/bilib/output.go#L11-L24

The race detector declares line 16 is racing with line 23

Line 23 is only called when the function under test has finished.

I think the problem is caused by the fact that there is a recursive call to `CaptureOutput` (you can see that in the second trace in the Details above).

When `fun()` has finished and before we run `return buf.Bytes()` the logger in use is still our new logger, but if another goroutine is using the previous logger (from the outer nested `CaptureOutput` call) does logging then it will use our `buf` (the inner nested one) at line 16 and cause the race when we run line 23  `return buf.Bytes()`.

So fixes might be - not to call `CaptureOutput` recursively.

Or re-arrange it something like this so that we stop the logger using `buf` before we read from it. 

```go
func CaptureOutput(fun func()) []byte {
	buf := &bytes.Buffer{}
	oldLevel := log.Handler.SetLevel(slog.LevelInfo)
	log.Handler.SetOutput(func(level slog.Level, text string) {
		buf.WriteString(text)
	})
	cleaned := false
	cleanup := func() {
		if cleaned {
			return
		}
		cleaned = true
		log.Handler.ResetOutput()
		log.Handler.SetLevel(oldLevel)
	}()
	defer cleanup()
	fun()
	cleanup()
	return buf.Bytes()
}
```

Or maybe simpler if we decide that a backtrace is unlikely

```go
func CaptureOutput(fun func()) []byte { 
 	buf := &bytes.Buffer{} 
 	oldLevel := log.Handler.SetLevel(slog.LevelInfo) 
 	log.Handler.SetOutput(func(level slog.Level, text string) { 
 		buf.WriteString(text) 
 	}) 
 	fun() 
	log.Handler.ResetOutput() 
	log.Handler.SetLevel(oldLevel) 
 	return buf.Bytes() 
 } 
```

Ok I took a closer look. Unfortunately the above ideas are still setting off the race detector, and I think it's because there is not actually a recursive call to `CaptureOutput`.  The only relevant call is here:

https://github.com/rclone/rclone/blob/231083647ef96fc97fd4dff6b88b93450a677693/cmd/bisync/bisync_test.go#L1217-L1219

so  I don't think `fun()` ever calls `CaptureOutput`. I think what you're seeing in the trace is the other parallel subtest, due to using `t.Parallel()` here:

https://github.com/rclone/rclone/blob/231083647ef96fc97fd4dff6b88b93450a677693/cmd/bisync/bisync_test.go#L310-L313

Here is a command to reproduce:

```
go test -run ^TestBisyncConcurrent$ github.com/rclone/rclone/cmd/bisync -race -v
```

```go
func CaptureOutput(fun func()) []byte {
	buf := &bytes.Buffer{}
	oldLevel := log.Handler.SetLevel(slog.LevelInfo)
	log.Handler.SetOutput(func(level slog.Level, text string) {
		buf.WriteString(text)
	})
	cleaned := false
	cleanup := func() {
		if cleaned {
			return
		}
		cleaned = true
		log.Handler.ResetOutput()
		log.Handler.SetLevel(oldLevel)
	}
	defer cleanup()
	fun()
	cleanup()
	// time.Sleep(5 * time.Second) // uncomment me to make the race more obvious
	return buf.Bytes()
}
```

Ultimately I think the ideal solution would be something like what I described [here](https://github.com/rclone/rclone/pull/8770#issuecomment-3223831943) about being able to pass a `ctx` and log each parallel subtest to a separate writer. But you are right that that would be a very large change. And while we could throw in mutexes, that would defeat the purpose of this test. 

I'm sort of wondering if maybe `TestBisyncConcurrent` should not bother trying to capture the Output, since all we're really looking for is to make sure the results are right and that the race detector didn't go off.

Is there a more elegant solution I'm not thinking of?
